# C++ Doxygen

Naming → [naming.mdc](naming.mdc). Structure → [structure.mdc](structure.mdc).

When a comment documents **API or a non-obvious contract** (params, returns, units, threading, side effects), use **Doxygen** on the declaration in `.hpp`. Short local “why” in `.cpp` stays plain `//`. Applies to **new / changed** code — do not mass-backfill legacy unless asked.

## When

| Situation | Form |
|-----------|------|
| Class / struct / enum / public method with params or contract | `/** ... */` with `@brief`, plus `@param` / `@return` / `@note` / `@warning` / `@throws` / `@ref` as needed |
| One-line description above a declaration | `/** @brief ... */` or `/// @brief ...` |
| Public package header | Optional `@file` + `@brief` at top |
| Local logic in `.cpp` (1–3 lines why) | `//` — not Doxygen |
| Name is enough, no contract | No comment |

Place docs on **declarations in `.hpp`**. Do not duplicate Doxygen blocks above definitions in `.cpp`. Blank line **before** the comment (see structure).

`@brief` must add information (units, thread-safety, failure modes) — do not restate the method name. Prefer tags used in husarz drivers: `@brief`, `@param`, `@return`, `@note`, `@ref`, `@file`, `@warning`, `@throws`. Do not require every tag on every method.

## Example

```cpp
/**
 * @brief Builds DALY response CAN ID for selected frame group and BMS index.
 * @param group DALY frame group selector.
 * @param bms_index BMS index in range 1..4.
 * @return 29-bit response CAN ID masked with CAN_EFF_MASK.
 */
inline constexpr uint32_t CreateResponseCanId(BmsFrameGroup group, uint8_t bms_index);

// Bad — prose without tags when documenting API
/* This function creates a response id from group and index... */
```
